From: Illia Komsa <ik@fungamer.co>
Subject: [PATCH] LibWeb: keep right-to-left text in order without wrapping and in numbers

The text chunker split a text node into direction runs only when its lines
wrap. Under white-space: nowrap or pre, a chunk holding Latin and Arabic was
shaped in its first code point's direction, so the Arabic rendered with its
letters (and words) in reverse visual order. Split direction runs in every
mode; without wrapping the split is no break opportunity, and the line box
places the runs in visual order (patch 0040), as it does when wrapping.

A number inside right-to-left text resolved to RTL and was shaped right to
left, so its digits came out reversed (UAX #9 I2 keeps them left to right).
Shape a run of European or Arabic digits, with only number separators and
terminators between them, left to right, and let the line box place it as one
unit of the right-to-left sequence.

A neutral run at a text node's edge, whose known side is not the embedding
direction, depends on text outside the node: leave it context-dependent for
the line box to resolve against the neighbouring fragments (patch 0040),
instead of joining the space before an Arabic word in a left-to-right
paragraph to the Arabic. The line box uses is_number_run and
number_punctuation_run to resolve the numbers between fragments.

---
